## 2. Related Work

Kovatsch *et al.* [2016] conducted the first large-scale passive measurement study of MQTT traffic on public brokers. Their work characterised topic naming conventions, payload types, QoS usage, and message rates across multiple brokers. Key findings included:

- The vast majority of messages carry plain-text or numeric payloads; JSON is a minority encoding.
- Almost all publishers use QoS 0 (at-most-once delivery).
- Topic hierarchies are shallow (median depth ≤ 3 levels) and topic strings are short (median ≤ 30 bytes).
- Traffic rates vary widely across brokers and exhibit daily periodicity.

Subsequent studies have documented the rise of JSON-structured payloads in newer deployments [Fernandes *et al.*, 2019] and have highlighted security concerns arising from unencrypted data exposed on public brokers [Nebbione & Calzarossa, 2020].

This work updates these findings to 2026 and introduces a geographic comparison enabled by the inclusion of a broker hosted in mainland China.

