## 3. Methodology

### 3.1 Broker Selection

Four publicly accessible MQTT brokers were selected, spanning two geographic regions:

| ID | Broker Name | Host | Region |
|----|-------------|------|--------|
| B1 | Eclipse IoT | `mqtt.eclipseprojects.io` | Europe |
| B2 | HiveMQ Public | `broker.hivemq.com` | Europe |
| B3 | EMQX Public | `broker.emqx.io` | Global |
| B4 | MQTTHQ | `public.mqtthq.com` | Asia (CN) |

All brokers offer unauthenticated access on port 1883. B4 is hosted in mainland China and was selected specifically to enable a geographic contrast. B1 replaces the `test.mosquitto.org` broker used in earlier studies due to improved availability.

### 3.2 Topic Subscription Strategy

Subscribing to the wildcard `#` at the root level is explicitly disallowed by most public brokers and their acceptable-use policies. The subscription list was therefore derived empirically from a broker topology dataset provided by the course instructor, comprising 4,207 broker profiles.

Three tiers of topic filters were constructed:

| Tier | Pattern type | Count | Example |
|------|-------------|-------|---------|
| **A** | `<root>/#` — the 40 most frequent root namespaces | 40 | `tasmota/#`, `gateway/#` |
| **B** | Exact full topic paths (top 25 by broker prevalence) | 25 | `ActiveMQ/Advisory/MasterBroker` |
| **C** | Legal catch-all: `+/#`, `+/+/#` (wildcard at level ≥ 2) | 2 | `+/#` |

This produces a subscription list of **67 topic filters**, all compliant with the no-root-wildcard constraint. All subscriptions were issued at **QoS 2** so that the `msg.qos` field of each received message reflects the *publisher's* original QoS level, according to MQTT's min(pub\_qos, sub\_qos) downgrade rule.

### 3.3 Data Collection

A multi-threaded Python collector (built on `paho-mqtt`) established simultaneous connections to all four brokers and logged every incoming message to a compressed pickle archive (`captured_messages.pkl.gz`). Each record stores the following fields, mirroring the schema of [Kovatsch *et al.*, 2016]:

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| `timestamp` | Unix epoch of message arrival |
| `broker` | Broker identifier (B1–B4) |
| `region` | Geographic region of the broker |
| `topic` | Full topic string |
| `topic_depth` | Number of non-empty topic levels |
| `topic_length` | Byte length of the topic string |
| `qos` | QoS level as published |
| `retain` | Retain flag set by publisher |
| `payload_length` | Byte size of the payload |
| `payload_type` | Classified as `string`, `json`, `numeric`, `bool`, or `unknown` |
| `payload_head` | First 20 bytes of the payload |

Payload classification follows the heuristic of [Kovatsch *et al.*, 2016]: boolean literals, numeric scalars, well-formed JSON objects/arrays, printable ASCII strings, and binary/unknown blobs are identified in that priority order.

### 3.4 Measurement Schedule

Captures were performed once per day for seven consecutive days, each lasting exactly **60 minutes**, for a total observation window of **7 hours** spread over one week. This design was chosen to balance temporal coverage — capturing possible daily variation in IoT traffic patterns — with the practical constraints of an academic laboratory setting. All capture sessions were initiated at the same time of day to limit confounding from circadian usage patterns.

